. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         // Our preferred measure of support for gender equality is plh0308_v2
> :
.         // "It’s best if the man and the woman work the same amount so they c
> an share the responsibility for taking care of the
.         // family and household equally" - with higher values indicating stro
> nger agreement and therefore greater support for equality
. 
.         // As detailed in the preregistration, we restrict the estimation to 
> fathers (i.e., male respondents with at least one child) 
.         // We use a simple linear regression, controlling for the number of k
> ids, assuming that child sex and thereby whether respondent has
.         // a daughter is as good as random conditional on the number of child
> ren (noting that decisions to have further children 
.         // may depend on the sex composition of those already born, which mak
> es this assumption somewhat questionable.)
.         
.         // A few standard controls are included to increase precision; howeve
> r, contrary to what we preregistered, we do not include 
.         // religion because of too many missing values
. 
.         // Fit separate regressions for fathers and mothers, weighted with th
> e cross-sectional weight phrf (as preregistered)
.         // We have to use iweights here for use with suest, but that does not
>  affect point estimates and SEs will be reestimated
.         // by suest anyway
. 
.         // Note: suest encountered a "base variable conflict" here when using
>  "i.agegr", apparently because there is no
.         // complete-case father in the 18-24 group in our test sample, hence 
> the switch to "ib1.agegr"
.         reg plh0308_v2 anydaughter i.nkids ib1.agegr i.edu i.agefrstbrthgr if
>  sex == 1 [iw=phrf]

